Looking at this problem, I need to determine the minimum number of isoclinic rotations to map any 2-plane in ℝ⁴ to a coordinate 2-plane.

## Proof

**Setup: Plücker coordinates and the self-dual/anti-self-dual decomposition.**

Identify ℝ⁴ with the quaternions ℍ. Every 2-plane through the origin corresponds to a point in the Grassmannian Gr(2,4), representable via Plücker coordinates $p_{ij}$ ($1 \le i < j \le 4$) satisfying the Plücker relation $p_{12}p_{34} - p_{13}p_{24} + p_{14}p_{23} = 0$.

Decompose $\Lambda^2 \mathbb{R}^4$ into self-dual and anti-self-dual 3-dimensional subspaces (eigenspaces of the Hodge star with eigenvalues $+1$ and $-1$). Define:
$$s = (p_{12}+p_{34},\; p_{13}-p_{24},\; p_{14}+p_{23}), \quad a = (p_{12}-p_{34},\; p_{13}+p_{24},\; p_{14}-p_{23}).$$

The Plücker relation is equivalent to $|s|^2 = |a|^2$. After normalization, a 2-plane is represented by a pair $(s, a) \in S^2 \times S^2$ (unit vectors in $\mathbb{R}^3$).

**Action of isoclinic rotations.**

An isoclinic rotation in ℝ⁴ is either **left-isoclinic** (both rotation angles equal, $\alpha = \beta$) or **right-isoclinic** ($\alpha = -\beta$). These correspond to left- and right-multiplication by unit quaternions, respectively.

A direct computation (e.g., with $L_i: x \mapsto ix$ and $R_i: x \mapsto xi$) verifies:
- **Left-isoclinic** rotations fix $s$ and act as $\mathrm{SO}(3)$ on $a$: $(s, a) \mapsto (s, R_L\, a)$.
- **Right-isoclinic** rotations fix $a$ and act as $\mathrm{SO}(3)$ on $s$: $(s, a) \mapsto (R_R\, s,\; a)$.

This is because left-multiplication commutes with the right-quaternionic structure (which governs the self-dual part) and vice versa.

**Coordinate planes in $(s,a)$ coordinates.**

The six Euclidean orthonormal coordinate 2-planes have:
| Plane | $s$ | $a$ |
|-------|-----|-----|
| span{e₁,e₂} | $(1,0,0)$ | $(1,0,0)$ |
| span{e₁,e₃} | $(0,1,0)$ | $(0,1,0)$ |
| span{e₁,e₄} | $(0,0,1)$ | $(0,0,1)$ |
| span{e₂,e₃} | $(0,0,1)$ | $(0,0,-1)$ |
| span{e₂,e₄} | $(0,-1,0)$ | $(0,1,0)$ |
| span{e₃,e₄} | $(1,0,0)$ | $(-1,0,0)$ |

In every case, **both** $s$ and $a$ are coordinate axis vectors (elements of $\{\pm e_1, \pm e_2, \pm e_3\}$).

**One isoclinic rotation is insufficient.**

A single isoclinic rotation (left or right) changes only **one** of $s$ or $a$ while leaving the other fixed. For the image to be a coordinate plane, **both** $s$ and $a$ must be coordinate axis vectors. For a general 2-plane, neither $s_0$ nor $a_0$ is a coordinate axis vector (the set of planes with $s_0$ on a coordinate axis is a measure-zero subset of Gr(2,4)). Since one rotation can only adjust one of the two, it cannot make both into coordinate axis vectors. Hence **one isoclinic rotation does not suffice**.

**Two isoclinic rotations suffice.**

Given an arbitrary 2-plane with normalized $(s_0, a_0) \in S^2 \times S^2$:

1. Apply a **right-isoclinic** rotation $R_R$ mapping $s_0 \mapsto (1,0,0)$. This is possible since $\mathrm{SO}(3)$ acts transitively on $S^2$. The plane becomes $((1,0,0),\; a_0)$.

2. Apply a **left-isoclinic** rotation $R_L$ mapping $a_0 \mapsto (1,0,0)$. The plane becomes $((1,0,0),\; (1,0,0))$, which is the coordinate plane $\operatorname{span}\{e_1, e_2\}$.

Thus two isoclinic rotations (one right, one left) map any 2-plane to a coordinate plane.

**Two is the minimum.**

We cannot use two rotations of the same type, since left-isoclinic (resp. right-isoclinic) rotations form a subgroup—their composition is again a single isoclinic rotation of the same type, reducing to the one-rotation case. Therefore we need at least one of each type, giving a minimum of **2**.

This is consistent with the structure theorem $\mathrm{SO}(4) \cong (S^3 \times S^3)/\{\pm 1\}$: every rotation is a product of one left- and one right-isoclinic rotation, $\mathrm{SO}(4)$ acts transitively on $\mathrm{Gr}(2,4)$, and no proper subgroup (single isoclinic family) acts transitively.

## Answer

The minimum number of isoclinic rotations required is $\boxed{2}$.

